### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence
Installed OpenClaw.app 2026.10.10, real home directory (`~/.claude/projects` has 23 directories at depth 2 or less; the Claude Desktop session store exists with 536 files in 3 directories; counts taken when the data was collected), same worker process throughout, normal desktop use of the Mac:

- Worker CPU: 4.15-7.95% of one core per 60 s window, mean 6.15% over 19 windows taken 10 minutes apart (`ps` cumulative CPU time deltas; values in Logs). RSS 341-549 MB. The earlier windows include exec traffic routed through that node during the investigation, and the dashboard state was not recorded per window, so these figures are an upper bound for the idle cost.
- `sample <pid> 8`, taken twice: `__getattrlist` 150 and 128, `lstat` 61 and 40, `__getdirentries64` 5 and 5. These are consistent with the per-directory identity probes of a tree poll.

Packaged debug build of `ec702e83388` on the same laptop, beside the installed app (separate profile, its own worker pid, bundled Bun 1.4.4). One `anthropic.claude.sessions.list.v1` call (20 rows), then three 60 s windows starting 150 s later, with no further reads:

- Worker CPU: 2.02% of one core (windows 1.95 to 2.08).
- 8 s `sample`, top of stack: `lstat` 99, `__getattrlist` 7. The worker keeps probing the trees with no reader.
- A build that closes idle polling watches, measured the same way, idles at 0.06% (0.03 to 0.10) with no probe frames at the top of the stack. That puts the cost of the polling at about 2 points of one core on this laptop.

There is no never-listed control from that run: another client on the same Gateway listed Claude sessions while the control windows were running, so those windows were armed too.

The polling explains about 2 points of the 4.15-7.95% seen on the installed worker, not all of it. The base debug build idled at 2.0% after one list, so the rest of the earlier figure did not reproduce there and is not attributed here. In the source repro, closing the idle watch takes the same 960-transcript case from 1.51% to 0.09%; the laptop also runs the Desktop-store watch.

### Impact and severity
- Affected: macOS app users whose node worker (Bun) has listed Claude sessions at least once. Hosts whose watches are event-backed (Node with fs-safe's native addon) are not affected. Node hosts where fs-safe falls back to polling (addon missing or disabled, or FSEvents admission failure) behave the same way as Bun.
- Severity: annoying. Steady background CPU and battery use. No data risk.
- Frequency: always, after the first list. It lasts until the worker restarts.
- Consequence: two directory trees are rescanned every second on the worker's main thread for its whole lifetime.

### Additional information
Last known good: NOT_ENOUGH_INFO. The same fs-safe version and watch code have been in place since at least 2026.10.10.
Related: #192144 changes the retry backoff clock in the same function. That is a separate change, and it does not affect this behavior.

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

On macOS, every SQLite connection open blocks on a synchronous `mount` child process to pick the journal mode, even for local APFS state where the chosen `wal` mode is already correct.

### Steps to reproduce

1. macOS, OpenClaw main at `57ab01ea`, state on the local APFS data volume.
2. Save the counting `mount` wrapper below as `~/f2-bench/shim/mount` and `chmod +x` it (any directory outside `/var` works; the commands below use `~/f2-bench`).
3. From the repo root, either run the `open-bench.mts` script below (40 real database opens through `configureSqliteConnectionPragmas`, command in its section), or boot the Gateway twice:

   ```sh
   pnpm install && pnpm build  # the bench boots dist/entry.js
   B=$HOME/f2-bench; mkdir -p "$B/tmp"
   # The bench child env drops OCPERF_MOUNT_LOG, so the shim reads its log path from current-log.
   : > "$B/boot.log"; echo "$B/boot.log" > "$B/shim/current-log"
   # TMPDIR outside /var keeps the bench state root canonical, like the default ~/.openclaw.
   PATH="$B/shim:$PATH" TMPDIR="$B/tmp" node --import tsx scripts/bench-gateway-startup.ts \
     --case default --runs 2 --warmup 0 --timeout-ms 120000
   wc -l < "$B/boot.log"
   ```

4. The last line prints the `mount` count (one line per call).

### Expected behavior

A database on local APFS keeps WAL without spawning a process on every open. `src/infra/sqlite-wal-filesystem.ts` already trusts the native, read-only APFS probe (`probeTreeClone`) to keep WAL when `mount` times out (#154492), so that evidence is available without `mount`.

### Actual behavior

`resolvePathJournalPolicy` -> `combineMountEntryJournalPolicies` -> `readMountEntries` forks `mount` on every call (no `/proc/self/mountinfo` on macOS, and `statfs` magic numbers cannot name APFS or smbfs). Measured on a Mac Studio (macOS 27.2, Node 24.22.2), shared host, load average 31-44 across runs:

- 40 real database opens (main `57ab01ea`, load average 32.6): **40 `mount` forks** in each of two runs, open p50 13.40 ms and 13.92 ms, journal_mode `wal`.
- Gateway boot (`scripts/bench-gateway-startup.ts`, built `dist` from main `6b959f4c`, which has the same `sqlite-wal-filesystem.ts`, canonical `TMPDIR`): 5-6 `mount` forks per 2 boots for `default`, 4 for the 12-agent `preparedRuntimeScaleMany` case (6 per 2 `default` boots on `57ab01ea` with the steps above).
- Every later agent database open adds one more fork.

### OpenClaw version

2026.11.1 (main `57ab01ea`; boot counts from `6b959f4c`, no change to the affected file in between)

### Operating system

macOS 27.2 (arm64)

### Install method

Source checkout, `pnpm install` + `pnpm build`

### Model

N/A (no model involved)

### Provider / routing chain

N/A

### Additional provider/model setup details

N/A

### Logs

```text
base: opens=40 mountForks=40 journal_mode=wal p50=13.40ms p90=16.77ms total=663.5ms load1=32.6 classify canonical: wal mountForks=1 classify /tmp alias: wal mountForks=1 classify extra: rollback mountForks=1
base: opens=40 mountForks=40 journal_mode=wal p50=13.92ms p90=15.31ms total=571.8ms load1=32.6 classify canonical: wal mountForks=1 classify /tmp alias: wal mountForks=1 classify extra: rollback mountForks=1
```

(`extra` is a real SMB share; it classifies as `rollback`, which is correct.)

### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

Counting wrapper for `mount` (save as `~/f2-bench/shim/mount`, `chmod +x` it, put its directory first on `PATH`):

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# The boot bench child env drops custom variables, so the log path falls back
# to a current-log file next to this shim.
echo "$(date +%s) $PPID" >> "${OCPERF_MOUNT_LOG:-$(cat "$(dirname "$0")/current-log")}"
exec /sbin/mount "$@"
```

<details><summary><code>open-bench.mts</code> (save in the repo root and run from there)</summary>

```sh
: > "$HOME/f2-bench/mount.log"
PATH="$HOME/f2-bench/shim:$PATH" OCPERF_MOUNT_LOG="$HOME/f2-bench/mount.log" node --import tsx open-bench.mts 40 [<smb mount>]
```

```ts
// Real SQLite opens through configureSqliteConnectionPragmas, then live classification.
// Run from the repo root: PATH=<shim dir>:$PATH OCPERF_MOUNT_LOG=<log> \
//   node --import tsx open-bench.mts [opens=40] [extra dir to classify, e.g. an SMB mount]
import fs from "node:fs";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { performance } from "node:perf_hooks";

const [opensArg = "40", extraDir] = process.argv.slice(2);
const src = (file: string) => path.resolve("src/infra", file);
const { requireNodeSqlite } = await import(src("node-sqlite.ts"));
const { configureSqliteConnectionPragmas } = await import(src("sqlite-wal.ts"));
const { resolvePathJournalPolicy } = await import(src("sqlite-wal-filesystem.ts"));
const mountLog = process.env.OCPERF_MOUNT_LOG!;
const forks = () =>
  fs.existsSync(mountLog) ? fs.readFileSync(mountLog, "utf8").split("\n").filter(Boolean).length : 0;

// realpath: macOS os.tmpdir() sits behind the /var -> /private/var symlink.
const root = fs.realpathSync(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "f2-open-bench-")));
const { DatabaseSync } = requireNodeSqlite();
const opens = Number(opensArg);
const before = forks();
const times: number[] = [];
const modes = new Set<string>();
for (let i = 0; i < opens; i++) {
  const databasePath = path.join(root, `agent-${i % 4}`, "state.sqlite");
  fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(databasePath), { recursive: true });
  const t = performance.now();
  const db = new DatabaseSync(databasePath);
  const maintenance = configureSqliteConnectionPragmas(db, { databasePath, checkpointIntervalMs: 0 });
  times.push(performance.now() - t);
  modes.add(String((db.prepare("PRAGMA journal_mode;").get() as { journal_mode: string }).journal_mode));
  maintenance.close();
  db.close();
}
times.sort((a, b) => a - b);
const ms = (q: number) => times[Math.floor(opens * q)]!.toFixed(2);
console.log(
  `opens=${opens} mountForks=${forks() - before} journal_mode=${[...modes].join(",")} ` +
    `p50=${ms(0.5)}ms p90=${ms(0.9)}ms total=${times.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0).toFixed(1)}ms ` +
    `load1=${os.loadavg()[0]!.toFixed(1)}`,
);

// Live classification. No database file is created on these paths.
const alias = path.join("/tmp", `${path.basename(root)}-alias`);
fs.symlinkSync(root, alias);
const targets: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ["canonical", path.join(root, "agent-0", "state.sqlite")],
  ["/tmp alias", path.join(alias, "agent-0", "state.sqlite")],
];
if (extraDir) targets.push(["extra", path.join(extraDir, "not-created", "state.sqlite")]);
for (const [label, target] of targets) {
  const f0 = forks();
  console.log(`classify ${label}: ${resolvePathJournalPolicy(target)} mountForks=${forks() - f0}`);
}
fs.unlinkSync(alias);
fs.rmSync(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
```

</details>

### Impact and severity

- Affected: macOS installs (any BSD without `/proc/self/mountinfo` takes the same path), every SQLite connection open: state DB, agent DBs on first use, workers, CLI processes.
- Severity: performance. Each open blocks the thread that opens the database for a `fork`+`exec` of `mount`. One `execFileSync("mount")` measured 5.08-6.00 ms in 5 fresh processes at load average about 33, and costs more on a busier host.
- Frequency: always.
- Consequence: 4-6 extra blocking `mount` forks per 2 Gateway boots and one per later DB open (boot wall time was too noisy on this shared host to quantify). The same dependency on `mount` is what turned a slow `mount` during a Time Machine backup into a failed Gateway start in #155927.

### Additional information

Related: #101108 (closed, cached the mount table with a 10 s TTL; review asked for real macOS proof and flagged freshness of a journal-safety decision), #155927 and #154492 (mount timeout fallback to the native APFS probe), #109448 and #109516 (mount probe timeouts). Linux reads `/proc/self/mountinfo`, which does not fork, and is not affected.

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

Every `readConfigFileSnapshot` runs a sync filesystem case probe for each configured agent, although the probe can only matter for agent directories that are equal ignoring case.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Check out `main` at `ee00ba6be092965acb14c82268348e4403c6e26d`, then run `pnpm install` and `pnpm build`.
2. Save the script below as `probe.mjs` outside the checkout. It writes a temporary config with `AGENTS` agents (default agent directories), patches the sync `fs` functions, runs 3 warm-up reads and then `N` timed `readConfigFileSnapshot()` reads from `dist/io.runtime.js`, and prints the sync fs calls per read.
3. From the checkout root, run `AGENTS=32 N=60 node /path/to/probe.mjs "$PWD"` (agent directories missing) and `AGENTS=32 N=60 EXISTING=1 node /path/to/probe.mjs "$PWD"` (agent directories created). Repeat with `AGENTS=2` and `AGENTS=8`.
4. Observe 544 `lstat`, 96 `stat` and 64 `readdir` per read at 32 agents (17 + 3 + 2 per agent; 608 `lstat` and 64 `readdir` with existing dirs), growing linearly with agent count, although no two agent directories are case variants of each other.

<details><summary>probe.mjs</summary>

```js
// Counts sync fs calls per readConfigFileSnapshot() for a config with AGENTS agents.
// Usage, from a built openclaw checkout (pnpm build): AGENTS=32 N=60 [EXISTING=1] node probe.mjs "$PWD"
import fs from "node:fs";
import { syncBuiltinESMExports } from "node:module";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { performance } from "node:perf_hooks";

const repo = path.resolve(process.argv[2] ?? ".");
const root = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "openclaw-probe-"));
const home = path.join(root, "home");
const state = path.join(home, ".openclaw");
fs.mkdirSync(state, { recursive: true });
const agents = Array.from({ length: Number(process.env.AGENTS ?? 8) }, (_, i) => `a${i}`);
const entries = Object.fromEntries(agents.map((id) => [id, { name: id }]));
fs.writeFileSync(
  path.join(state, "openclaw.json"),
  JSON.stringify({
    gateway: { mode: "local", port: 18999, auth: { mode: "none" } },
    agents: { ownership: "explicit", entries },
  }),
);
if (process.env.EXISTING) {
  for (const id of agents) {
    fs.mkdirSync(path.join(state, "agents", id, "agent"), { recursive: true });
  }
}
process.chdir(root);
process.env.HOME = home;
process.env.OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR = state;
process.env.OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH = path.join(state, "openclaw.json");

let counting = false;
const counts = {};
for (const name of ["readFileSync", "statSync", "lstatSync", "readdirSync", "openSync", "realpathSync"]) {
  const orig = fs[name];
  fs[name] = function (...args) {
    if (counting) counts[name] = (counts[name] ?? 0) + 1;
    return orig.apply(this, args);
  };
  if (orig.native) fs[name].native = orig.native;
}
syncBuiltinESMExports();

const io = await import(path.join(repo, "dist/io.runtime.js"));
const N = Number(process.env.N ?? 60);
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) await io.readConfigFileSnapshot();
const times = [];
counting = true;
for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
  const start = performance.now();
  await io.readConfigFileSnapshot();
  times.push(performance.now() - start);
}
counting = false;
times.sort((a, b) => a - b);
const perCall = Object.entries(counts).map(([k, v]) => [k, +(v / N).toFixed(1)]);
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  agents: agents.length,
  existing: Boolean(process.env.EXISTING),
  medianMs: +times[N >> 1].toFixed(3),
  syncFsPerCall: Object.fromEntries(perCall),
}));
fs.rmSync(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
```

</details>

### Expected behavior

The probe result is used only to choose between a resolved path and its lowercased form (`src/config/agent-dirs.ts:33`: `return isPathCaseInsensitive(resolved) ? normalizeLowercaseStringOrEmpty(resolved) : resolved;`). It can therefore only change the duplicate check for directories that are equal ignoring case. A config whose agent directories all differ ignoring case should not need any case probe.

### Actual behavior

One full case probe per agent per read. Sync fs calls per `readConfigFileSnapshot`, from the steps above on a `pnpm build` of `ee00ba6b`. I ran the steps twice and got the same counts both times:

| Agents | Agent dirs | lstat | stat | readdir |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 2 | missing | 34 | 6 | 4 |
| 8 | missing | 136 | 24 | 16 |
| 32 | missing | 544 | 96 | 64 |
| 2 | existing | 38 | 0 | 4 |
| 8 | existing | 152 | 0 | 16 |
| 32 | existing | 608 | 0 | 64 |

At 32 agents the median read took 6.3 to 7.7 ms on a shared host (load average 24 to 43). All of it is sync work on the event loop.

### OpenClaw version

main at ee00ba6be092965acb14c82268348e4403c6e26d (2026-11-05)

### Operating system

macOS 27.2 (arm64), APFS. The code path is platform-independent.

### Install method

Source checkout (`pnpm install`, `pnpm build`)

### Model

Not applicable (config validation, no model call)

### Provider / routing chain

Not applicable

### Additional provider/model setup details

None.

### Logs

{"agents":32,"existing":false,"medianMs":6.27,"syncFsPerCall":{"realpathSync":4,"readFileSync":1,"lstatSync":544,"statSync":96,"readdirSync":64}}
{"agents":32,"existing":true,"medianMs":7.741,"syncFsPerCall":{"realpathSync":4,"readFileSync":1,"lstatSync":608,"readdirSync":64}}

### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

The counts came out the same in two runs.

### Impact and severity

- Affected: every Gateway with more than one agent. The cost grows linearly with the number of agents.
- Severity: low to medium. A few milliseconds of sync I/O per read.
- Frequency: always, on every config snapshot read with 2 or more agents.
- Consequence: each `readConfigFileSnapshot` spends this sync I/O on the event loop (measured above). Per the source, `config.get`, config writes and reloads go through it.

### Additional information

Related correctness history, none of them duplicates: #105194, #105655, #106582 and #127913 (false collisions on case-sensitive volumes), #105402 (current probe-based folding), #105442 (closed, per-component keying) and #134511 (shared probe helper). No existing issue or PR covers the per-agent probe cost.

prs.body:
Related: #112760 (closed as not planned).

[steipete’s closing comment](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/112760#issuecomment-6380197979) says current main resolved the original list-absence symptom and an explicit access-revoked signal is an optional follow-up, requiring a concrete current UI failure for a fresh issue. This PR proposes that optional contract; whether to accept it remains a maintainer decision.

## What Problem This Solves

This PR proposes an explicit Control UI signal when a sharing change changes a viewer’s access to an open session. A missing row in a filtered session list is not an access decision.

## User Impact

The open composer immediately becomes unavailable after access is revoked or changes to read-only, and recovers when shared access returns. Suggest mode continues to allow a suggestion draft until access is revoked. Missing list rows remain neutral. No configuration or data migration is required.

## Why This Change Was Made

Publish a connection-specific `session.access.changed` event after a committed visibility or membership change. The closed payload contains only `agentId`, `sessionKey`, `sessionId`, and `access` (`revoked`, `read-only`, or `read-write`). Publication uses the canonical sharing target and current recipient authority; no-op, denied, stale, and unrelated-recipient changes do not publish an access fact.

Control UI stores restrictions for the admitted connection and exact session instance. A restoration retires the matching restriction; existing session-row and scope checks still decide whether an action is allowed. Composer, pending typing, Talk, read-marker, and dashboard-default writers consume that state. Disconnect or connection replacement retires it. This is additive: older clients may ignore the event; native composer behavior, authorization policy, persistent schema, and protocol version are unchanged.

