Closes #193840

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: config reads, writes and reloads on multi-agent Gateways block the event loop on filesystem work that grows with agent count.

## User Impact

User impact: multi-agent Gateways spend less sync I/O on every config snapshot read (`config.get`, config writes, reloads and startup); the measured paths are the `config.get` cache miss and the watcher's re-read. At 32 agents each config snapshot read makes 192 fewer `lstat`, up to 96 fewer `stat` and 64 fewer `readdir` calls. Duplicate agent directory detection reports the same collisions as before. There is no config or behavior change.

## Why This Change Was Made

Case folding can only merge two directories whose resolved paths are equal ignoring case. `findDuplicateAgentDirs` now resolves all agent directories first and runs `isPathCaseInsensitive` only for agents whose lowercased path matches another agent's. Every other agent keeps its resolved path as its key, and no folded key can equal that key, so the reported collision groups and their order are unchanged. Candidates still get the same per-path probe as before, which keeps the case-sensitive volume fix from #105402 intact.

This removes the probe calls instead of caching them, so there is no new cache key to invalidate. I considered a per-volume memo and rejected it. Case sensitivity can differ between directories on the same device (Linux casefold, Windows per-directory case sensitivity), so a `dev` key could change answers.

## Evidence

### Isolated Gateway, `config.get` through the shipped CLI

Separate `pnpm build`s of base `14d9d554` and this change, on the same Mac Studio (APFS, case-insensitive). Each run starts its own `openclaw gateway run` with a temporary `HOME`, state dir and config, a loopback port and `auth: none`. It is not the live Gateway. The config has 32 agents with distinct existing `agentDir`s and `gateway.reload.mode: "off"`. A preload hook (`NODE_OPTIONS=--import fs-count.mjs`) counts sync `fs` calls in every thread of the Gateway process. The CLI processes are not counted. Each sample is one `openclaw gateway call config.get --port <port> --json`.

`config.get` caches its response per config revision while the config watcher is live, and the watcher stays live with `reload.mode: "off"`. A repeated call on an unchanged file is therefore a cache hit. Each repeated sample is an edit cycle instead: rewrite the config (rename agent `a0`), wait 1.5 s, then call `config.get`. Every call returned the edited name, so each one was a cache miss.

Four rounds in the order base, fix, base, fix, 10 edit cycles per run. Mean sync fs calls in the Gateway process:

| Sample | lstat | readdir | stat |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `config.get` after an edit (cache miss) | 514.0 to 515.0 -> 322.3 to 323.1 | 64.0 to 64.1 -> 0 to 0.1 | 5 to 5.4 -> 5 |
| First `config.get` after start | 514 to 522 -> 322 | 64 -> 0 | 5 to 7 -> 5 to 7 |
| Watcher work after each edit (it re-reads the config) | 973 to 986 -> 779 to 793 | 65 -> 1 | 71 -> 71 |
| Cached `config.get` (5 calls) | 2.8 to 7.6 -> 2.8 to 4.4 | 0 -> 0 | 0 to 0.4 -> 0 to 0.4 |
| Idle control, 1.9 s windows | 0 to 2.3 -> 0 to 1.3 | 0 -> 0 | 0 to 0.7 -> 0.7 |

