Closes #193858
Related: #101108, #155927, #154492, #172220

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: every SQLite connection open on macOS blocks on a `mount` child process when state is on local APFS.

## User Impact

User impact: on macOS, Gateway boot and every state, agent and worker database open stop spawning `mount` for local APFS stores. Journal-mode decisions are unchanged: network, FUSE/SSHFS, cross-VM and unclassifiable stores keep the rollback or refusal policy they have today. No config, migration or user action.

## Why This Change Was Made

`statfs` magic numbers cannot name APFS on macOS, so `resolvePathJournalPolicy` fell through to `execFileSync("mount")` on every open. The module already trusts a read-only native APFS probe (`probeTreeClone`) to keep WAL when `mount` times out (#154492). This change runs that probe first for canonical paths and uses `mount` only when the probe cannot confirm APFS.

Unlike #101108 (closed), nothing is cached: no TTL, no stale mount table after a NAS remount, no remembered timeout.

What stays on `mount` metadata, unchanged:
- aliased paths (original path differs from the real path), because both locations need mount metadata (existing rule from #154492);
- any volume the probe does not report as APFS (smbfs, nfs, macfuse/osxfuse, unknown), with one extra native probe (well under the cost of the `mount` fork) before the same `mount` read;
- a failed probe; then the existing 1 s timeout and rollback fallback apply.

Accepted edge case: a symlinked state directory, or a database under an unresolved macOS `$TMPDIR` path (`/var` -> `/private/var`), still forks `mount` per open, as today.

Test-line budget: `config/test-lines-allocations/sqlite-wal-apfs-before-mount.txt` grants the net +40 regression-test lines. Reason: prove canonical APFS skips `mount` while aliased paths still read it. The shared baseline is unchanged.

## Evidence

Mac Studio (macOS 27.2, arm64, Node 24.22.2), shared host, load average 31-44 across all runs, so operation counts are the primary evidence. The `mount` count comes from a wrapper first on `PATH` that appends a line per call and execs `/sbin/mount` (script below).

<details><summary>First-open cost and overlap with #172220</summary>

First-open cost: the probe loads the `@openclaw/fs-safe` native binding on the first darwin open, where base loaded it only after a `mount` timeout. Measured in 5 fresh processes (load average about 33): first probe 2.38-2.55 ms, second 0.057-0.070 ms, one `execFileSync("mount")` 5.08-6.00 ms. The first open therefore costs less than the fork it replaces.

Overlap: open #172220 (gVisor 9p, Linux-only) edits `combineMountEntryJournalPolicies` and `sqlite-wal.test.ts`. There is no semantic conflict, but whichever lands second needs a textual rebase.

</details>

